# ML Project Conventions

## Repo Structure

- Phase code lives in `phase{N}-{name}/week{N}-{name}/`
- Write-ups live in `docs/`
- Never commit model weights, checkpoints, or data files (see `.gitignore`)
- Push notebooks after every session; commit message should name the lecture or concept

## Code Style

- Python 3.11+; use type hints on all function signatures
- Every new file gets a top-of-file docstring: purpose, and a short usage example
- Prefer explicit over implicit — no magic imports, no star imports
- Use `pathlib.Path` instead of `os.path` string manipulation
- Prefer functional code over OOP where possible — plain functions, pure transforms, and explicit data flow. ML libraries (e.g. `nn.Module`) require OOP; our own code should default to functions unless a class clearly earns its keep

## Notebooks

- Each notebook has a title cell (markdown) with: lecture name, date, key concept
- Keep cells short and linear — one concept per cell block
- Clear all outputs before committing (reduces diff noise)
- Name files: `{lecture-slug}_{short-description}.ipynb` e.g. `lecture1_micrograd.ipynb`

## Experiments

- Log every training run to W&B with at minimum: model name, dataset, lr, batch size, epochs
- Save hyperparameters as a config dict at the top of training scripts, not scattered inline
- Always set a random seed for reproducibility (`torch.manual_seed(42)`)
